**Claim.** Yes, such a sequence exists.

**Proof.** Since $(E,d)$ is a Polish space, it is in particular a standard Borel space: $(E,\mathcal{B}(E))$ is Borel-isomorphic to a Borel subset of $[0,1]$, and $\mathcal{B}(E)$ is countably generated.

**Construction of the probability space.** Set
$$\Omega := E^{\mathbb{N}} = \prod_{n=1}^{\infty} E,$$
the countable product of copies of $E$. Equip $\Omega$ with the product $\sigma$-algebra
$$\mathcal{F} := \mathcal{B}(E)^{\otimes \mathbb{N}} = \bigotimes_{n=1}^{\infty} \mathcal{B}(E).$$
Because $E$ is Polish (hence separable and metrizable), the countable product $E^{\mathbb{N}}$ is itself Polish under a compatible complete separable metric (e.g. $D(\omega,\omega') = \sum_n 2^{-n}\min(1,d(\omega_n,\omega'_n))$), and the product $\sigma$-algebra $\mathcal{B}(E)^{\otimes\mathbb{N}}$ coincides with $\mathcal{B}(E^{\mathbb{N}})$, the Borel $\sigma$-algebra of the product topology.

**Existence of the product measure.** We construct the countable product probability measure $P = \mu^{\otimes\mathbb{N}}$ on $(\Omega,\mathcal{F})$. Consider the finite-dimensional cylinder sets
$$\mathcal{C} := \bigcup_{N=1}^{\infty}\Bigl\{\pi_N^{-1}(B) : B\in \mathcal{B}(E)^{\otimes N}\Bigr\},\qquad \pi_N(\omega)=(\omega_1,\dots,\omega_N).$$
This is an algebra generating $\mathcal{F}$. Define a set function $P_0$ on $\mathcal{C}$ by
$$P_0\bigl(\pi_N^{-1}(B)\bigr) := \mu^{\otimes N}(B),\qquad B\in\mathcal{B}(E)^{\otimes N}.$$
This is well defined: if a cylinder set admits two representations $\pi_M^{-1}(B_M)=\pi_N^{-1}(B_N)$ with $M\le N$, then $B_N = B_M\times E^{N-M}$, and by the consistency of finite product measures $\mu^{\otimes N}(B_N)=\mu^{\otimes M}(B_M)$. Finite additivity of $P_0$ on $\mathcal{C}$ is immediate.

It remains to verify countable additivity (equivalently, continuity from above at $\varnothing$) of $P_0$ on $\mathcal{C}$, so that the Carathéodory extension theorem yields a unique extension $P$ to $\mathcal{F}=\sigma(\mathcal{C})$. This follows from the **Kolmogorov extension theorem** (or equivalently the Ionescu–Tulcea theorem applied to the constant kernel $\mu$): since each factor $(E,\mathcal{B}(E),\mu)$ is a probability space and the finite-dimensional distributions $\mu^{\otimes N}$ are consistent under projections, there exists a unique probability measure $P$ on $(\Omega,\mathcal{F})$ whose pushforward under every $\pi_N$ is $\mu^{\otimes N}$. Concretely, the Ionescu–Tulcea theorem builds $P$ iteratively from the regular conditional distributions (here all equal to $\mu$), and since $\mu$ is a probability measure on a standard Borel space, no topological regularity beyond measurability is required. Thus $P=\mu^{\otimes\mathbb{N}}$ is a well-defined probability measure on $(\Omega,\mathcal{F})$.

**Definition of the random variables.** For each $n\in\mathbb{N}$, let $X_n:\Omega\to E$ be the $n$-th coordinate projection:
$$X_n(\omega) := \omega_n,\qquad \omega=(\omega_1,\omega_2,\dots)\in\Omega.$$
Each $X_n$ is $\mathcal{F}/\mathcal{B}(E)$-measurable by definition of the product $\sigma$-algebra.

**Distribution.** For any $B\in\mathcal{B}(E)$,
$$P(X_n\in B)=P\bigl(\{\omega:\omega_n\in B\}\bigr)=P\bigl(\pi_n^{-1}(E^{n-1}\times B\times E^{\mathbb{N}\setminus\{n\}})\bigr).$$
This is a cylinder set whose finite-dimensional base at level $n$ is $E^{n-1}\times B$, which under $\mu^{\otimes n}$ has measure $\mu(B)$. Hence $P(X_n\in B)=\mu(B)$, i.e.
$$P\circ X_n^{-1}=\mu\quad\text{for all }n\in\mathbb{N}.$$

**Independence.** Let $n_1<\cdots<n_k$ be distinct indices and $B_1,\dots,B_k\in\mathcal{B}(E)$. Set $N=n_k$ and define
$$\widetilde B := \prod_{i=1}^{N} C_i\in\mathcal{B}(E)^{\otimes N},\qquad C_{n_j}=B_j\ (j=1,\dots,k),\quad C_i=E\ \text{otherwise}.$$
Then $\{X_{n_j}\in B_j,\ j=1,\dots,k\}=\pi_N^{-1}(\widetilde B)$, so
$$P\bigl(X_{n_1}\in B_1,\dots,X_{n_k}\in B_k\bigr)=\mu^{\otimes N}(\widetilde B)=\prod_{j=1}^{k}\mu(B_j)=\prod_{j=1}^{k}P(X_{n_j}\in B_j).$$
By the $\pi$–$\lambda$ theorem (or the definition of independence via finite-dimensional distributions), the family $\{X_n:n\in\mathbb{N}\}$ is independent.

**Conclusion.** On the probability space $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},P)=\bigl(E^{\mathbb{N}},\mathcal{B}(E)^{\otimes\mathbb{N}},\mu^{\otimes\mathbb{N}}\bigr)$, the coordinate projections $X_1,X_2,\dots$ are independent $E$-valued random variables, each with distribution $\mu$. Therefore such a sequence always exists. $\blacksquare$

